        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 825,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 691,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Applies the exponentially scaled modified Bessel function of order 0 - I0(x) times e to the minus |x| - to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is I0 for large inputs: wherever I0 would overflow (a von Mises distribution with a high concentration, say), work with this and keep the exponential factor separately, or in logs.\n\nTHE CURVE:\n1 at 0, symmetric, falling slowly on both sides, about like 1 over the square root of 2 pi |x|: 0.47 at 2, 0.128 at 10, 0.04 at 100 - where I0 itself is already infinite in 32-bit floats.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.i0e\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined and finite for every real number.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-10 to 10 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is I0e(x) of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -10 to 10, drawn from 0 (bottom) to 1.05 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.i0 is the unscaled function. t.special.i1e is the order-1 version, and t.special.scaled_modified_bessel_k0 the same trick for the decaying K0.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 675,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Applies the exponentially scaled modified Bessel function of order 0 - I0(x) times e to the minus |x| - to every element of a tensor.\n\nIt is I0 for large inputs: wherever I0 would overflow (a von Mises distribution with a high concentration, say), work with this and keep the exponential factor separately, or in logs.\n\nTHE CURVE:\n1 at 0, symmetric, falling slowly on both sides, about like 1 over the square root of 2 pi |x|: 0.47 at 2, 0.128 at 10, 0.04 at 100 - where I0 itself is already infinite in 32-bit floats.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.i0e\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nA tensor - or a number or a list, which is turned into one. Receiving it triggers the node. Each element is worked on separately, so the output is the same shape as the input. Whole-number input comes out as floats.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, element by element, in the same shape. On the same device as the input; float32 in gives float32 out.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nDefined and finite for every real number.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second. t.linspace makes 200 evenly spaced values from its start to its stop (-10 to 10 here), and the upper plot draws them: a straight ramp. The lower plot is I0e(x) of each of those 200 values, so it is the graph of the function from -10 to 10, drawn from 0 (bottom) to 1.05 (top). Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look at another stretch.\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.i0 is the unscaled function. t.special.i1e is the order-1 version, and t.special.scaled_modified_bessel_k0 the same trick for the decaying K0."
        },
